response.text:
The key simplification is to prove a link through the **registered shift flag**, rather than reason directly about every ALU-control state. These lemmas are intended for a reset-reachable proof. The target `prop` has no explicit clock, so its assertion must be checked with the same `posedge clock` event. ([fpga.mit.edu](https://fpga.mit.edu/6205/_static/F25/documentation/1800-2017.pdf))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clock) disable iff (rst) ((I679 || I680 || I681) |-> qShiftRight); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Entry into an active control state records ShiftRight in qShiftRight. While active, ShiftRight and qShiftRight are both high, so mShiftRight prevents further control-register updates. This establishes the control-state-to-shift-flag link."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clock) disable iff (rst) (qShiftRight |-> (qINSo[2:1] == 2'b10)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "When qShiftRight first becomes high from idle, the triggering instruction has INS[2:1] equal to 2'b10, which makes the sampled INSo[2:1] equal to 2'b10. If qShiftRight remains high, either those registers hold or the flag clears. Together with lemma_1, this implies prop and closes the one-step invariant."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

I’ll trace the control updates and identify a compact inductive invariant.The key is the nesting: as written, the control registers and `qINSo` update only in the `else if (LDMQ)` branch, when `mShiftRight` is false. The lemma below captures the condition that prevents an active control state from changing. Both the lemma and the target should be checked on `posedge clock`; concurrent assertions sample values before that edge’s procedural updates. ([accellera.org](https://www.accellera.org/images/resources/videos/SystemVerilog_Assertions_Tutorial_2016.pdf?utm_source=openai))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clock) disable iff (rst) (I679 || I680 || I681) |-> qShiftRight; endproperty",
      "explanation": "When any control flag is set, ShiftRight is 1. The lemma makes mShiftRight 1 regardless of TESTMODE, so the branch that updates the flags and qINSo cannot execute. It is 1-inductive: the only transition from all flags clear to an active flag requires INS=3'b100, which also captures ShiftRight=1 into qShiftRight. On that transition, INSo[2:1]=2'b10 is captured into qINSo; afterward both the flags and qINSo hold."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

The large ALU datapath is not needed for this proof. The key is the control update path: once a control bit is active, `mShiftRight` prevents further updates to both the control bits and `qINSo`. Entry into that active state supplies the required instruction decode.

**Clocking note:** `prop` has no clock event in its declaration. The lemmas below use `posedge clock`; assert `prop` with the same clock, or add that event to its declaration. A property declaration does not acquire a module’s default clock merely by being declared there. ([fpga.mit.edu](https://fpga.mit.edu/6205/_static/F25/documentation/1800-2017.pdf))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clock) disable iff (rst) (I679 || I680 || I681) |-> qShiftRight; endproperty",
      "explanation": "An active control state is entered only from the idle state when I744 is asserted. ShiftRight is then asserted and captured in qShiftRight. While active, ShiftRight and qShiftRight are both asserted, so mShiftRight prevents the branch that updates the control state and qINSo from executing. This reachable-state invariant closes the hold case of the target property."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clock) disable iff (rst) (!(I679 || I680 || I681) && I744) |-> (INSo[2:1] == 2'b10); endproperty",
      "explanation": "I744 decodes the entry instruction INS == 3'b100. In the idle state I754 is low, making the combinational instruction decode 2'b10 regardless of I683 or MQ_q. Thus the update that first activates a control bit captures the required value in qINSo, closing the entry case."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

The target `prop` has no explicit clock event. Check it at `@(posedge clock)`, or provide a default clock, when proving it alongside this lemma. ([accellera.org](https://accellera.org/images/eda/sv-ec/8246.html?utm_source=openai))

